WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.170
- [Alexandra] When markets get choppy,

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:03.470
investors can pull out

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.940
to save their money, or
they can cast their lines

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.780
for discounted stock, aiming
for a bigger profit later on.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.700
This second option is
called buying the dip

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.960
and it's been a popular
strategy in the markets

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.150
for years now.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.600
It was even touted
during the crash of 2008.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.490
- This is not a time to
be selling your stocks.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.640
It's times like these, where
people really make money

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.730
because stocks become a better value.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.260
- [Alexandra] But so far
in 2022, buying the dip

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.190
hasn't paid off as well as in the past.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.710
Markets are volatile
and in this environment,

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.840
buying the dip can be risky.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.380
- What we're seeing is
that after people step in

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.200
to buy the stock market dip,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.580
the S&amp;P500 has kept falling the day after.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.540
So that means that you're
sitting on a loss pretty quickly.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.220
- [Alexandra] But even these circumstances

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.660
haven't stopped investors who
want to bring in a big catch.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.030
Here's why it's such
an attractive strategy

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.783
but nearly impossible to time.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.290
Let's zoom out, buying
the dip is when investors

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.080
purchase assets after they
have dropped in price,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.350
on the assumption that their
value will bounce back.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.141
- What we've seen over
the past few decades

00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:15.910
is that sharp downturns
in the stock market

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.780
have often been followed
by these huge rebounds.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.020
So people that have sat on the sidelines

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.340
during say the financial crisis

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.400
or the March 2020 COVID
crash, have watched stocks

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.070
kind of go up after those instances.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.560
- [Alexandra] Investors willing
to dive in, tend to have

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.930
one of three main motivations.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.300
First, some investors want to
buy into growing companies.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.010
They care less about the price
of the stock and more about

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.600
the opportunity to reel in a company

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.220
they think will experience
significant growth in the future.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.360
Other investors look for
stocks with upward momentum,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.320
meaning rising prices.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.130
They're most interested in
timing the bottom of the dip

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.330
so they can maximize profits

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.120
and then there's investors
who want cheap stocks.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.150
They don't care as much
about how fast and how far

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.290
their stocks will grow,
but they're excited

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.400
that stocks that were previously expensive

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.690
are now cheap enough for them to grab.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.980
Mainly they think the
stocks they're buying

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.300
will keep going up.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.720
Whatever their motivation,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.440
if a lot of investors
attempt this strategy

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.680
the result can have another effect,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.100
one that has played out
in the past few years

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.300
as more individual investors
have entered the market.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.970
- Individual investors have
become such a prominent force

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.810
in markets in recent years.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.830
They've stampeded into the markets,

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.030
they've bought a record amount of stocks.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.740
So some analysts and strategists do say

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.050
that they can kind of provide a backstop

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:45.110
to the market and help support
the market during volatility.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.880
- [Alexandra] Sometimes
when enough investors

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.380
try to catch cheap stocks,

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.290
they can actually reverse
the downward trajectory.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.920
Take for example, the 2008 crash,

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.910
after the housing bubble burst.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.900
It sent markets into a
tailspin, but by 2009,

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.410
many investors began
reentering the market,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.710
pushing up the prices of cheap stocks.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.210
- [Alexandra] Investors halted
a three day losing streak,

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.170
sending stocks broadly
higher on a weaker dollar

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.640
and better than expected
home sales numbers.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.110
- [Alexandra] It was known
as the 2009 Dash for Trash.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.550
- We saw that that prolonged
downturn and that steep selloff

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.820
was followed by an incredible recovery.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.490
An 11 year bull market for stocks.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.850
- [Alexandra] the MSCI
All Country World Index

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.610
ended up returning its
best annual performance

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.440
on record in 2009,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.180
just after its worst performance in 2008.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.710
- People who said the
stock market is too scary

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:39.830
for me right now,

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.020
they missed out on those
mammoth, mammoth gains

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.710
and may have regretted it.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.720
- [Alexandra] Strategists
say that since then,

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.800
investors have been
conditioned to buy the dip

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.930
and it's made individual investors money,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.270
at least until 2022.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.020
Since the Federal Reserve has made

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.800
the biggest interest
rate increase since 2000,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.810
some think a recession is ahead.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.000
- This is a huge shift
for financial markets

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.650
and that's one of the key reasons

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.470
we've seen this volatility.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.830
- [Alexandra] It's an example
of why it can be so hard

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.420
for investors to time the dip,

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.030
because stocks can keep on falling.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.020
Vanda research estimates the average

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.590
individual investor portfolio
peaked late in 2021,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:24.370
and then tumbled, losing an
average of about 28% by May 9th.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.320
With all the dangers
associated with buying the dip,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.420
it's no wonder that
analysts consider it risky.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.260
- If you choose to buy the dip,
you can't expect, you know,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.570
the market to bounce immediately.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.880
It could take days, weeks,
months, or even years

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:40.880
for it to rebound

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.643
and for you to pocket
a profit on that trade.

